HolRefute provides certified counterexample generation for HOL4.  It has a
Quickcheck (QC) backend for executable goals and a Kodkod-based model-finder
(MF) backend.  QC supports exhaustive and random strategies over three
interchangeable execution substrates:

* Compute interprets test plans with computeLib.  It is the compatibility
  substrate and supports registered custom and abstract generators.
* Cv compiles a fuel-bounded HOL test loop and executes it with
  Thm.compute.  Goals outside its first-order fragment are inapplicable.
* NativeSML extracts the executable fragment to SML and compiles the test
  loop in process.  It is the preferred fast substrate on Poly/ML.

Set a substrate with `Refute.upd_substrate`.  The default, `Auto`, walks all
registered substrates in priority order; the built-in order is NativeSML,
Cv, then Compute.  Auto falls through when a substrate is inapplicable.  An
explicitly selected inapplicable substrate reports `Unknown`; it never
silently changes substrate.  Selection applies to both exhaustive and random
QC backends.  A custom or replacement substrate can open a smart-generator
gate only by compiling the smart plan itself, and Auto executes that exact
selected compilation.  At Refute trace level 2 or higher, Auto reports
selection and fallthrough reasons.  Every counterexample also records the
selected substrate in its `substrate` field.

Random testing uses one specified 64-bit generator and consumption order on
all substrates.  `Refute.upd_seed (SOME n)` therefore reproduces the same
candidate stream independently of substrate.  Runs without an explicit
seed use a deterministic session stream which advances between runs.

Smart generators are enabled by default.  A `Hol_reln` premise, or a
Boolean function with exhaustive Horn-shaped equations, is mode-compiled to
a positive CPS enumerator for exhaustive testing on all three substrates.
`upd_depth` sets its fixed per-plan fuel (default 10); it does not add a
schedule loop.  Compute and cv define the same structurally recursive HOL
list enumerators (cv then translates those definitions); native SML preserves
the same clause and premise order.  Because fuel bounds every smart
enumeration, Enum- or SmartGuard-bearing terminal exhaustion is always
reported as incomplete.  Ignored counterexamples are matched by structural
environment equality, not by stream position.  Setting
`upd_smart_generators false` restores generate-and-guard planning, and
`upd_reorder_premises` controls mode-analysis reordering.

Native narrowing is active by default as the `narrowing` backend.  It uses
`size` as its refinement-depth bound and supports prenex existential goals.
`upd_finite_functions true` (the default) represents function inputs by
finite `ffun` update chains, with `cfun` as the constant-only fallback for
higher-order domains.  Counterexamples reconstruct `ffun` values as HOL
`UPDATE` chains.  Setting the option to false restores the direct function-
type inapplicability diagnostic.

Execution substrates are untrusted accelerators.  A QC candidate becomes
`Genuine` only after Refute_Cert re-evaluates its instantiated proposition
with computeLib and constructs a theorem certifying its negation.  The model
finder can also classify a sound finite model as `Genuine` when its
genuineness conditions hold even if the goal is not certifiable; in that
case `cert = NONE`.  Thus `certainty` records the semantic strength of the
result, while `cert` records whether Refute constructed a HOL theorem.
Refute uses no oracle tags.  Cv performs all per-call definitions and
translations inside a full theory snapshot/revert bracket.  Success,
failure, timeout, and interruption paths leave no Refute-created type,
constant, theorem, or binding in the user's theory; if safe cleanup is
unavailable, Cv reports that the goal is inapplicable instead.

Backend races stop only when a result reaches the highest certainty that
any selected backend declares reachable for the current configuration and
instances.  `Refute.register_backend` gives existing backends the safe,
conservative ceiling `Genuine`.  A backend with a tighter bound can use
`Refute.register_backend_with_ceiling`.  Its callback must return an upper
bound: overestimating only forgoes an early stop, whereas underestimating can
hide a stronger result and violates the backend contract.

The unused-assumption facade consists of `check_unused_assms`,
`find_unused_assms`, and `print_unused_assms`.  Each takes a `config option`
first.  `NONE` derives a QC-only configuration from the current configuration;
`SOME cfg` supplies the base configuration and preserves its backend selection.
Every individual probe forces a sequential, quiet, abort-potential,
expectation-free profile.

Module layout:

* Refute_Core: shared types, configuration, registry, and orchestration.
* Refute_Gen: generator derivation and enumeration support.
* Refute_Eval: the substrate seam, registry, and common random generator.
* Refute_EvalCompute, Refute_EvalCv, and Refute_EvalSML: substrates.
* Refute_Extract: in-memory HOL-to-SML extraction for NativeSML.
* Refute_QC: QC test planning and random/exhaustive execution.
* Refute_Cert: EVAL-based counterexample certification.
* Refute_Forl and Refute_ForlSat: the FORL/Kodkodi process bridge and SAT
  solver configuration.
* Refute_ModelFinder and Refute_ModelFinder_*: preprocessing, scope and
  representation selection, Kodkod translation, and model reconstruction.
* Refute_Unused: unused-assumption probes and theory sweeps.
* Refute.sig and Refute.sml: the public API facade.

M4 model-finder surface
-----------------------

The M4 model finder adds native polymorphic scopes, monotonicity and smart
finitization, specialization and boxing, binary integers, (co)inductive
predicates, codatatypes, quotients, typedefs, value pinning, model formatting,
and incremental multi-model search.  The new or newly active options and their
defaults are:

| updater | default | meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `upd_specialize` | `true` | specialize static higher-order arguments |
| `upd_box` | `[(NONE, NONE)]` | box functions and pairs when useful |
| `upd_binary_ints` | `NONE` | select binary integers when useful |
| `upd_bits` | `[1, ..., 10]` | candidate binary word widths |
| `upd_star_linear_preds` | `true` | use exact closure for linear predicates |
| `upd_iter` | default depth row (below) | fixpoint unrolling depths |
| `upd_bisim_depth` | `[9]` | codatatype bisimulation depth; `~1` disables it |
| `upd_finitize` | `[(NONE, NONE)]` | finitization selected by monotonicity |
| `upd_whack` | `[]` | constants whose definitions are ignored |
| `upd_need` | `NONE` | values that must occur in the model |
| `upd_tac_timeout` | `0.5` seconds | inner tactic timeout |
| `upd_format` | default row `[1]` | displayed model-value depth |
| `upd_max_potential` | `1` | maximum potential models returned |
| `upd_max_genuine` | `1` | maximum genuine models returned |
| `upd_merge_type_vars` | `false` | fuse all MF type-variable scopes |

The default `iter` row is `[0,1,2,4,8,12,16,20,24,28]`.  A `NONE` key in a
row is its fallback.  For smart Boolean rows, a `NONE` value asks Refute to
choose; `SOME true` and `SOME false` force the choice.
The `max_potential` and `max_genuine` limits are global budgets.  Requesting
more than one model forces an incremental SAT solver when necessary, and a
genuine result ends the potential-model search.  With
`upd_merge_type_vars true`, the model finder type-instantiates every goal,
evaluated-term, and needed-term type variable to the alphabetically first one
before building its context.  This reduces independent scope blocks, at the
cost of potentially losing models that require the variables to have
different cardinalities.

Registrations are session-local ML state and create no theory content:

* `Refute.register_codatatype {tyop, case_const, constructors}` registers a
  genuine type operator, its case constant, and its distinct constructors.
  Registration checks the constructor and branch types and requires a unique
  codatatype scrutinee argument; both scrutinee-first and scrutinee-last case
  constants are supported.  Lazy built-ins cover `llist`, `ltree`, `itree`,
  `itreeTau`, `lbtree`, and `path` when their theories are present.
* `Refute.register_quotient {qty, rty, abs, rep, equiv_thm, partial}`
  registers the abstract and representation types and constants.  The
  theorem shape determines whether the sound partial encoding is required;
  the legacy `partial` field is retained for source compatibility.
* `Refute.register_typedef {ty, abs, rep, absrep_thm}` registers an existing
  HOL typedef.  `absrep_thm` must be its exported conjunction of the two
  bijection laws and must agree with the type's `<tyop>_TY_DEF` theorem.

All registration types use distinct type variables as parameters.  Abs, rep,
case, and constructor terms must be existing constants.  A later compatible
registration for the same type operator replaces the session entry.

Kodkodi support is Unix-only and requires a Java runtime.  Obtain
`kodkodi-1.5.7.tar.gz` from the Isabelle components mirror at
<https://isabelle.in.tum.de/components/> and unpack it locally.  Set
`HOL4_KODKODI` to the resulting `kodkodi-1.5.7` component directory.  Its
`jar/` directory must contain `antlr-runtime-3.1.1.jar`, `kodkod-1.5.jar`,
`kodkodi-1.5.7.jar`, and `sat4j-2.3.jar`.

HolRefute constructs the Kodkodi JVM command itself.  Java is selected from
`HOL4_JAVA_EXECUTABLE`, then `JAVA_HOME/bin/java`, then `java` on `PATH`.
As an escape hatch, `HOL4_KODKODI_EXEC` can instead name a complete
standalone launcher command.  It takes precedence and is passed to the
shell verbatim.  Configuration checks are lazy and never start a JVM while
modules are loaded or built.  The launcher shipped in the component's
`bin/` directory depends on Isabelle and is not the standalone override.

`SAT4J` is the portable default.  JNI solvers are advertised only when their
individual shared libraries are present under `jni/<platform>`: MiniSat,
CryptoMiniSat, and Lingeling require `libminisat`, `libcryptominisat`, and
`liblingeling`, respectively.  External solver settings retain Isabelle's
environment variables, including `MINISAT_HOME`, `CRYPTOMINISAT_HOME`,
`ZCHAFF_HOME`, `RSAT_HOME`, `RISS3G_HOME`, `BERKMIN_HOME`, and
`BERKMINALLOY_HOME`; `BERKMIN_EXE` selects a nondefault BerkMin executable.

The prepared development host uses
`$HOME/.local/opt/Isabelle2025-2/contrib/kodkodi-1.5.7` and
`$HOME/.local/bin/java`; other machines need a local Kodkodi 1.5.7 component
and a Java runtime.  No network access is needed at run time.

HolRefute is included in the normal tree build only on Poly/ML; non-Poly
builds skip the directory.  Its level-2 selftests include the MF acceptance,
differential, soundness, and theory-hygiene suites, plus cross-substrate QC
outcome and candidate-stream conformance and Cv export-cleanliness checks.

The refute_simp, refute_psimp, and refute_unfold theorem sets are defined by
the support theory and used by model-finder preprocessing.

`Refute.upd_iter` selects model-finder unrolling depths.  A mutual fixpoint
group shares one iterator row: members are checked in `_cases` theorem order,
and the first member with an explicit row wins before the default row is used.
Predicates introduced by `Hol_reln` and `Hol_coreln` are recognized through
the IndDef registries and support direct well-founded equations or joint
least/greatest-fixpoint unrolling.  Hand-rolled greatest fixpoints are not
recognized; define coinductive predicates with `Hol_coreln`.

Known limitations and follow-ups
--------------------------------

An Abs/Rep occurrence for an unregistered typedef makes the model finder
return `Unknown`, rather than risk a spurious genuine result.  Register the
typedef explicitly.  The `rat` type is not registered: its intended
fraction/ersatz representation remains future work.

Remaining model-finder parity work includes fraction/`rat` support and
monotonicity promotion for relation inverse and composition (`inv` and `O`).
